You classify one settled decision of a software delivery loop: where, if anywhere, it belongs in the
repository's knowledge base. Reply with one JSON object and nothing else.

## The kinds you may answer

- `adr`: a decision record — how something is built, and why. Fields: `title`, `statement`, `reason`.
- `invariant`: something that must always hold in the code. Fields: `place`, `statement`, `enforcedBy` (optional), `reason`.
- `rule`: a precise, provable business rule. Fields: `place`, `statement`, `serves` (an existing principle's id, of `product` or of the rule's own place, or `new`), `principle` (`{ statement, why }`, only when `serves` is `new`), `enforcedBy` (optional), `reason`.
- `covered`: an existing entry or decision record already says this. Fields: `covers` (its id, or `ADR-NNNN`), `reason`.
- `stays-here`: a local choice with nothing lasting to keep; it stays in the ledger. Fields: `statement`, `reason`.

`place` is `product`, or one of the existing domains below; never a new one.
`statement` and `principle`'s fields are one or two plain sentences, at most 300 characters.
`reason` says why this kind and this place, at most 200 characters.
Prefer `covered` when the knowledge base below already says it, and `stays-here` for a local choice.
`enforcedBy`, on a `rule` or an `invariant` only, names one to 3 of the files listed under "The files the feature pull request changed": the ones whose tests or constraints prove the statement. Omit `enforcedBy` when none of them proves the statement.
An entry states what the product does and guarantees, never how it looks: no colour, size, layout, position, count of visual elements, font, or exact label or copy. A candidate that is only about the look stays local. A candidate that mixes both is written as the behaviour alone.

## The decision: s1-01-two-snapshots

### The question

Should the check read its settings from the pull request itself?

### The decision

It reads its settings from the base branch, so a pull request cannot rename the override label.

### The options (A is what was built)

A. Read the settings from the base branch, the option built.
B. Read them from the pull request.

### What the agent had to decide

Where `evaluate` reads the config from.

### What it did meanwhile

It reads base settings and head delivery as two snapshots.

### What it costs to change later

One argument.

### The answer (verdict: agreed)

Yes, keep it.

## The knowledge base

### Domains

- billing: # Billing
- shipping: # Shipping

### Principles

- P-PRODUCT-1 (product): A person reviews every change before it reaches the default branch.
- P-BILLING-1 (billing): An invoice is never changed once sent.

### Decision records

- ADR-0001: ADR-0001 — The outbox check runs as an app

### Rules and invariants

- BR-PRODUCT-1 (rule, product): No bot writes to the default branch.
- N-BILLING-1 (invariant, billing): Every invoice carries a number.

## The files the feature pull request changed

(none)
